WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.260
It's great to welcome back to the program
today, Dr. Michael Shermer, who's the publisher

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:08.250
of Skeptic magazine and a New York Times best
selling author.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:14.540
His latest book is Giving the Devil His Due
Reflections of a Scientific Humanist.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:16.299
It's so great to have you back on.

00:00:16.299 --> 00:00:17.330
Nice to be back on, David.

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:18.349
Nice to see you.

00:00:18.349 --> 00:00:20.610
So these are strange times.

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:23.689
And I mean, just just to sort of open things
up.

00:00:23.689 --> 00:00:30.380
What have the last couple of months revealed
to you, if anything, about humanity that you

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:36.930
maybe did not consider as deeply before in
terms of our ability to deal with calamity,

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:38.780
about our leaders, about really anything?

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:41.079
I mean, just where is your mind right now?

00:00:41.079 --> 00:00:46.860
Yeah, I'd say first of all, people for the
most part have been pretty good about social,

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:47.860
social isolation.

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:51.690
And, you know, staying down in one place.

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:56.500
You know, there's a few handful of protesters
with their Confederate flags or swastikas

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:01.059
and and semiautomatic rifles that people point
to.

00:01:01.059 --> 00:01:02.550
But that's pretty rare.

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:06.720
I think most people, particularly in California
where I'm at, are pretty good.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:14.810
On the negative side, I'd say it's a sign
of how fragile and networked the economy is.

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:19.061
Quickly, things can kind of unravel and there's
no mystery about it.

00:01:19.061 --> 00:01:21.650
We know exactly why the economy has collapsed
in.

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:27.370
But it does show you to what extent it it's
all sort of propped up by all of us doing

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:28.600
our thing.

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:34.380
You know, just kind of collective action by
individual action and how what happens when

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:38.450
everybody stops, even when they don't want
to.

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:39.890
How fast that can unravel.

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:43.390
Although, as Adam Smith famously said, there's
a lot of ruin in a nation.

00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:48.490
So I don't think we're on the brink of the
apocalypse or anything like that.

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:54.759
But obviously, we have to get back to work
at some point here in the first probably bit

00:01:54.759 --> 00:02:01.090
by by June or July, certainly by the end of
the summer before things really get pretty

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:04.520
serious in that regard just based on the numbers.

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:10.860
I'd say I'm a little bit worried this morning
that the death rate has not plummeted like

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:12.860
I thought it would when the testing went up.

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:19.790
I thought with mass testing, the denominator
would go way up and therefore the percentage

00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:22.150
